 favorite um  you know  dvds  for example  although my all-time favorite was just  toki romance which to me was the  ultimate exotic russian experience on  the volga 19th century uh some guy from  georgia and so forth but there's a whole  bunch of them this is another great one  you know this was  irony  which is a classic in russian in russia  they watch it apparently every new  year's  uh  i got into some of the uh  some of the more modern ones but i find  that the modern russian there's also  brag somewhere  uh  here we have  but the modern russian movies are a  little more violent the old russian  soviet movies you know  uh  here we have another one  these were great they had a got to get  my glasses on my skull  whatever  and  so that was that however another major  and and i'll give you a bit of a tour  now then maybe of my library  i did want to give you a little bit of  an overview of some of the material that  i have used  for my russian  here for example atseiji  was one of my favorites i listened to it  over and over again the same is true of  yama by cuprin  but these of course were later on  the very first let me see the very first  audiobook that i did was  um the kreutzer sonata croissant sonata  by tolstoy  i did the whole assimil series but of  course as i'll show you when i get to  the books that was only one of the  um ser you know learning materials that  i used  uh of course i watched a lot of russian  videos i went and got some of these dvds  just to display them here for you  uh one of my favorites was sabachi  cersei  you know vinaimir  uh  i mean i could go on and on my favorite  was just okie romance which i wasn't  able to find in a hurry here these are  some of the cds that i listened to  um you know and i have only displaced  some of them because i've got a whole  bookshelf  full of these cds so that's just the  sort of dvd  cd uh section when i say listening and  reading i include reading so i have a  lot of books here  in russian including  books for learning russian and books for  me to read in russian  and i'm sure i have others elsewhere in  my library  of course that's a bit messy here we're  doing work in the house this is my  korean section my german section  and so forth at least of my uh  cds because  the dvds the movies were more to to  encourage and stimulate and motivate me  and it was fun but the learning was from  listening to  the audiobooks and then reading them on  link and saving words and phrases and  accumulating this total of 90 000 some  odd  known words  granted in you know the way there are so  many different forms of the same word  you can't just  start beating your chest and saying i  know 90 000 words but it indicates that  i've done a lot of of reading and where  i've read i've largely  listened  but a major impact on my russian  learning  aside from the tremendous help i got  from evgeny and other tutors there was a  while i should have shown that in my  statistic  there was a while where i was doing a  lot of talking as well because i mean  we're talking about a long period of  engagement with russian so perhaps two  years i didn't speak but then for  another couple of years i'd be on  talking to  elena to  jenny to other tutors number of tutors  that i had linked tutors  and and speaking and learning about  russia and learning russian and it was  just a wonderful wonderful experience  and  but a major influence was eccomas creek  now echomaski is a radio station in  moscow  not very popular in russia  because it's somewhat anti-regime  although in fact there's quite a wide  range of opinion there they have like  you know zuganov the communist is there  you've got uh  uh prokhorov i can't remember it's been  a while now i haven't been listening to  it for a while but you had there's this  imperialist guy  and they have a sort of a  wide range of opinion  i wouldn't call them moderate in any way  they're all very you know emphatic in  what they have to say but if you listen  to a a group of these people you get a  sense of what people are talking about  in russia the big advantage of echo must  be is that every day there are  i don't know a dozen interviews  audio with text so every day  i would import  the text into link i wouldn't bother  importing the audio because i would have  that on my  you know iphone or whatever but i go  through and mine it for words and  phrases i mean i did that for years  every day i would load up with five  interviews i'd be listening in the car  listening everywhere reading  echo was a major  uh influence on  me developing my russian  now russians get on say ah that's uh you  know they're all  american stooges and blah blah blah and  whatever but in fact it's that's not  quite fair there is a variety but as  with any  uh information source you have to pick  and choose and you can't just accept  things at face value and  i don't accept everything at face value  that i hear on that chemistry  however it helped me a great deal  so so basically my experience with  russian was one of discovering first of  all the classics  because there i was able to buy went to  riga latvia to buy  all of these all of these uh  cds i got lots of them which you don't  need to do now because you can find them  online  uh  and i bought all these dvds movies which  you don't need to do now because you can  find them in netflix you can find them  in youtube  um  and and so i was exploring that aspect  of russia but at the same time through  ecommerce i was exploring  daily events russian perspectives on  things happening around the world  russia-us relations russia ukraine  relations russia china relations and so  i was getting a a rather broad base the  other thing that russia did for me is it  got me into the slavic languages it was  my first slavic language  and of course  yeah it is difficult  it is difficult because there are many  things in russian that we don't have in  english we don't have  uh you know we're verbs 